input.minimal_fix:
File: /output/test2.rs — delete line 16 (and the now-redundant blank line after it). Keep the explanatory comment on lines 14-15.

Replace:

    // `int main(int argc, char* argv[])` accepts arguments but never reads
    // them, so the output is the same for any command line.
    let _args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();

    let mut bar = ProgressBar::new();

with:

    // `int main(int argc, char* argv[])` accepts arguments but never reads
    // them, so the output is the same for any command line.
    let mut bar = ProgressBar::new();

That is the whole change — one deleted statement. No other edits are needed: `std::env` is not referenced anywhere else in the crate (verified by grep over test2.rs and src/), so nothing breaks and no unused-import warning appears. Verified: builds warning-free under both `cargo build --release` and bare `rustc -O test2.rs`, and afterwards the no-arg, valid-UTF-8-arg, and non-UTF-8-arg cases all exit 0 and emit the same 45 bytes as /workspace/dataset/test2_executable.

Do NOT substitute `std::env::args_os()` here — it would silence the panic but reintroduce a pointless allocation to model a value the C++ demonstrably never touches. args_os() is only the right answer if some future code actually needs the arguments.

